## Proof

The map $g: TM \to T^*M$ is the musical isomorphism (the "flat" operator): it sends $(p, v) \in TM$ to $(p, v^\flat) \in T^*M$, where $v^\flat = g_p(v, \cdot) \in T^*_p M$. This is a bundle map covering the identity on $M$. We show it is an isometry by computing its differential and checking it preserves the Sasaki metrics.

### Step 1: The differential of $g$ on vertical vectors

A vertical vector at $(p,v) \in TM$ arises from varying $v$ while holding $p$ fixed. For $w \in T_pM$, the vertical lift $w^v_{(p,v)}$ is tangent to the curve $t \mapsto (p, v + tw)$. Applying $g$ gives $t \mapsto (p, g_p(v+tw, \cdot)) = (p, v^\flat + t\, w^\flat)$, whose tangent at $t=0$ is the vertical lift of the one-form $w^\flat$ at $(p, v^\flat)$. Thus:

$$dg_{(p,v)}(w^v) = (w^\flat)^v.$$

### Step 2: The differential of $g$ on horizontal vectors

The horizontal distribution on $TM$ is defined by the Levi-Civita connection $\nabla$ on $TM$, and the horizontal distribution on $T^*M$ is defined by the dual connection $\nabla^*$ on $T^*M$. The horizontal lift $X^h_{(p,v)}$ is tangent to the curve $t \mapsto (c(t), P_t v)$, where $c'(0)=X$ and $P_t$ is $\nabla$-parallel transport along $c$.

Applying $g$ yields $t \mapsto (c(t),\, g_{c(t)}(P_t v, \cdot))$. We claim this is the $\nabla^*$-parallel transport of $v^\flat$ along $c$, i.e., $g_{c(t)}(P_t v) = P_t^*(v^\flat)$. This follows from metric compatibility of the Levi-Civita connection: for any vector field $Y$ along $c$,

$$\frac{d}{dt}g_{c(t)}(P_t v, Y) = g(\nabla_{\dot c} P_t v, Y) + g(P_t v, \nabla_{\dot c} Y) = g(P_t v, \nabla_{\dot c} Y),$$

since $P_t v$ is parallel. On the other hand, the dual connection satisfies $(\nabla^*_{\dot c}\alpha)(Y) = \dot c(\alpha(Y)) - \alpha(\nabla_{\dot c}Y)$, so $\alpha_t = g_{c(t)}(P_t v, \cdot)$ satisfies

$$(\nabla^*_{\dot c}\alpha_t)(Y) = \frac{d}{dt}g(P_t v, Y) - g(P_t v, \nabla_{\dot c}Y) = 0.$$

Hence $g_{c(t)}(P_t v)$ is $\nabla^*$-parallel, confirming the image curve is the horizontal lift of $X$ at $(p, v^\flat)$. Thus:

$$dg_{(p,v)}(X^h) = X^h.$$

### Step 3: Metric preservation

Since $dg$ maps horizontal to horizontal and vertical to vertical (with $w \mapsto w^\flat$ on the vertical part), and both Sasaki metrics split orthogonally into horizontal and vertical components, it suffices to check each block.

**Horizontal–horizontal:**
$$\bar g\bigl(dg(X^h),\, dg(Y^h)\bigr) = \bar g(X^h, Y^h) = g(X,Y)\circ\pi = \hat g(X^h, Y^h). \quad\checkmark$$

**Vertical–vertical:** Using $g^{-1}(X^\flat, Y^\flat) = g^{ij}(X^\flat)_i(Y^\flat)_j = g^{ij}g_{ik}X^k\, g_{jl}Y^l = g_{kl}X^k Y^l = g(X,Y)$:
$$\bar g\bigl((X^\flat)^v,\, (Y^\flat)^v\bigr) = g^{-1}(X^\flat, Y^\flat)\circ\pi = g(X,Y)\circ\pi = \hat g(X^v, Y^v). \quad\checkmark$$

**Horizontal–vertical:**
$$\bar g\bigl(dg(X^h),\, dg(Y^v)\bigr) = \bar g(X^h, (Y^\flat)^v) = 0 = \hat g(X^h, Y^v). \quad\checkmark$$

### Conclusion

The differential $dg$ preserves the inner product on all three blocks (horizontal–horizontal, vertical–vertical, horizontal–vertical). Therefore $g: (TM, \hat g) \to (T^*M, \bar g)$ is an isometry.

